## A temple built for two

Every Egyptian temple belongs to a god. Kom Ombo belongs to two. Built under the Ptolemies and finished under Rome, it is doubled from front to back: two gateways side by side, two columned halls, two dark sanctuaries at the end. The northern half is for Horus the Elder, the falcon; the southern half for Sobek, the crocodile god of the river. Walk down the centre line and you can feel the symmetry on either side of you.

## The crocodile god of the river

Sobek was lord of the Nile's waters and its crocodiles, feared and honoured in equal measure. At Kom Ombo, where crocodiles once basked on the sandbanks below the temple, he had an important sanctuary. Beside the temple, the small Crocodile Museum holds mummified crocodiles found nearby, the god's sacred animals, laid to rest with care. It's the moment the temple's story becomes real.

> Two gods shared this temple for centuries. At a quiet moment, you have it all to yourselves.

## Above the river

The setting is half the reason to come. The temple stands on a low rise right above a bend in the Nile, and with the river still and the steps empty, the warm sandstone and the water hold you there longer than you planned. When to arrive is left to your concierge, with local knowledge and our craft. On the Original day, your concierge takes you there and gives you the time to see it; on the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read the double temple.

## On the Temple Road

Kom Ombo is about three hours south of Luxor and under an hour north of Aswan. From Luxor, it's easiest as part of our Temple Road day, which takes in Esna, Edfu and Kom Ombo and ends in Aswan before dusk, with a €150 road supplement for the one-way drive. If you're staying in Aswan, it makes an easy, unhurried morning.
